B.TN=new A.a5("Lateral epicondylitis (tennis elbow) involves which muscle/tendon?",B.O6,1,'Lateral epicondylitis primarily involves the extensor carpi radialis brevis (ECRB) tendon at the lateral epicondyle. Despite being called "tennis elbow," it is more common in non-tennis activities involving repetitive wrist extension and gripping.')
B.OH=s(["Wrist extensors","Wrist flexors and pronators","Supinators","Triceps"],t.s)
B.TD=new A.a5("Medial epicondylitis (golfer's elbow) involves the origin of which muscle group?",B.OH,1,"Medial epicondylitis affects the common flexor-pronator tendon origin at the medial epicondyle. The flexor carpi radialis and pronator teres are most commonly involved. It is important to assess for ulnar nerve irritation, as the nerve runs in the cubital tunnel just posterior to the medial epicondyle.")
B.Ma=s(["Radial tunnel","Cubital tunnel","Carpal tunnel","Guyon's canal"],t.s)
B.UU=new A.a5("The ulnar nerve passes through which anatomic space at the elbow?",B.Ma,1,"The ulnar nerve passes through the cubital tunnel (between the medial epicondyle and olecranon). It is the second most common upper extremity nerve entrapment (after carpal tunnel syndrome). Prolonged elbow flexion, direct pressure, and subluxation are risk factors.")
B.P8=s(["0-5 degrees valgus","5-15 degrees valgus","15-25 degrees valgus","25-35 degrees valgus"],t.s)
B.V0=new A.a5("The carrying angle of the elbow is normally:",B.P8,1,'The normal carrying angle is approximately 5-15 degrees of valgus (larger in women). An increased carrying angle (cubitus valgus) can put the ulnar nerve at risk. A decreased angle (cubitus varus, or "gunstock deformity") can result from a malunited supracondylar fracture.')
B.M4=s(["Lateral collateral ligament","Annular ligament","Ulnar (medial) collateral ligament","Interosseous membrane"],t.s)
B.V7=new A.a5("The primary stabilizer of the elbow against valgus stress is:",B.M4,2,"The ulnar collateral ligament (UCL/MCL), specifically its anterior band, is the primary stabilizer against valgus stress. UCL injuries are common in overhead throwing athletes. Tommy John surgery involves UCL reconstruction.")
